traces.inference_trace:
Strip the parentheses to get the digit string, then try every split point into a left part and a right part. For each part, generate all valid number representations, and combine every left option with every right option.

For a part `t` of length n, the valid representations are:
- The integer form `t` itself, allowed if it has no leading zero or is exactly "0".
- For each decimal position i from 1 to n-1, the form `t[0..i] + "." + t[i..]`. The integer part `t[0..i]` must not have a leading zero unless it is exactly "0". The fractional part `t[i..]` must not end in "0".

Checking against the examples:
- "0": the integer form is valid, and there is no decimal split.
- "00": the integer form has a leading zero, so it is invalid. The split "0.0" ends in zero, so it is invalid too. No options remain.
- "01": the integer form is invalid. The split "0.1" is valid.
- "10": the integer form is valid. The split "1.0" ends in zero, so it is invalid.

The output format has a space after the comma, and the examples' trailing spaces are artifacts, so I'll produce "(a, b)".

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public IList<string> AmbiguousCoordinates(string s) {
        string d = s.Substring(1, s.Length - 2);
        var res = new List<string>();
        for (int i = 1; i < d.Length; i++) {
            var left = Gen(d.Substring(0, i));
            if (left.Count == 0) continue;
            var right = Gen(d.Substring(i));
            foreach (var a in left)
                foreach (var b in right)
                    res.Add("(" + a + ", " + b + ")");
        }
        return res;
    }

    private List<string> Gen(string t) {
        var r = new List<string>();
        int n = t.Length;
        if (t[0] != '0' || n == 1) r.Add(t);
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            string ip = t.Substring(0, i);
            string fp = t.Substring(i);
            if (ip.Length > 1 && ip[0] == '0') continue;
            if (fp[fp.Length - 1] == '0') continue;
            r.Add(ip + "." + fp);
        }
        return r;
    }
}
```

