traces.inference_trace:
Sort the array and let `dp[i]` be the number of trees whose root is `arr[i]`. Every value can be a single-node tree, so `dp[i]` starts at 1.

For each `i`, loop over `j < i` with `arr[j]` as the left child. If `arr[i] % arr[j] == 0`, the right child is `arr[i] / arr[j]`. If that value is in the array at index `k`, add `dp[j] * dp[k]` to `dp[i]`. A dictionary from value to index gives the lookup. Because the left child is every possible divisor, ordered pairs are counted naturally.

The answer is the sum of all `dp[i]`, taken modulo 10^9 + 7. Use `long` for the products.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int NumFactoredBinaryTrees(int[] arr) {
        const long MOD = 1000000007;
        Array.Sort(arr);
        int n = arr.Length;
        var idx = new Dictionary<int, int>();
