traces.inference_trace:
Values are at most 1e5, so I use two Fenwick trees indexed by value, one for counts and one for sums. I also keep a queue of the last m elements.

- **Add:** enqueue the number and update both trees. If the queue size exceeds m, dequeue the oldest element and remove it from both trees.
- **Query:** if fewer than m elements have been seen, return -1. Otherwise the answer is (sum of the smallest m-k elements − sum of the smallest k elements) / (m − 2k).

To get the sum of the smallest t elements, descend the Fenwick tree by count to find the largest position pos whose prefix count is ≤ t. Take the prefix sum up to pos, then add (t − count) × (pos+1) for the remaining elements, which all have value pos+1.

Descent details: start with pos = 0, remaining = t, sum = 0. For step from the highest power of two (2^17 = 131072 > 1e5, so the tree size is 100001 and LOG is 17) down to 1, if pos+step ≤ N and cnt[pos+step] ≤ remaining, then pos += step, remaining −= cnt[pos], sum += sm[pos]. At the end, sum += remaining × (pos+1). If remaining is 0 this adds nothing, so no special case is needed.

Sums can reach 1e5 × 1e5 = 1e10, so I use long.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class MKAverage {
    const int N = 100000;
    long[] cnt = new long[N + 1];
    long[] sm = new long[N + 1];
    Queue<int> q = new Queue<int>();
    int m, k;

    public MKAverage(int m, int k) {
        this.m = m;
        this.k = k;
    }

    void Update(int v, int dc) {
        for (int i = v; i <= N; i += i & -i) {
            cnt[i] += dc;
            sm[i] += (long)dc * v;
        }
    }

    long SumSmallest(int t) {
        int pos = 0;
        long rem = t;
        long sum = 0;
        for (int step = 1 << 16; step > 0; step >>= 1) {
            int np = pos + step;
            if (np <= N && cnt[np] <= rem) {
                pos = np;
                rem -= cnt[np];
                sum += sm[np];
            }
        }
        sum += rem * (pos + 1);
        return sum;
    }

    public void AddElement(int num) {
        q.Enqueue(num);
        Update(num, 1);
        if (q.Count > m) {
            int old = q.Dequeue();
            Update(old, -1);
        }
    }

    public int CalculateMKAverage() {
        if (q.Count < m) return -1;
        long total = SumSmallest(m - k) - SumSmallest(k);
        return (int)(total / (m - 2 * k));
    }
}
```

