The examples in the statement contain trailing spaces (e.g. `"cbd "`), which look like formatting artifacts. To be safe, the code ignores any character outside `'a'..'z'` when computing f.

Approach:
1. Compute f for each word and build a count array `cnt[freq]` for frequencies 1 to 10 (11 slots to be safe).
2. Build a suffix sum `suf[k]`, the number of words with f > k, and answer each query by looking up `suf[f(query)]`.

Since the string length is at most 10, f is at most 10. A trailing space is not a lowercase letter, so it is skipped. I use an array of size 12 and a suffix array where `suf[i] = sum of cnt[j]` for `j >= i`. The answer for a query with frequency `q` is `suf[q+1]`, so the array needs index up to 12 (size 13).

```csharp
public class Solution {
    private int F(string s) {
        int[] c = new int[26];
        foreach (char ch in s) {
            if (ch >= 'a' && ch <= 'z') c[ch - 'a']++;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if (c[i] > 0) return c[i];
        }
        return 0;
    }

    public int[] NumSmallerByFrequency(string[] queries, string[] words) {
        int[] cnt = new int[13];
        foreach (var w in words) {
            cnt[F(w)]++;
        }
        int[] suf = new int[14];
        for (int i = 12; i >= 0; i--) {
            suf[i] = suf[i + 1] + cnt[i];
        }
        int[] res = new int[queries.Length];
        for (int i = 0; i < queries.Length; i++) {
            int q = F(queries[i]);
            res[i] = suf[q + 1];
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

