3. For each stone i, and each k in its set, try steps k-1, k, k+1 with step > 0. If `stones[i] + step` is a stone at index j, add `step` to `sets[j]`.
4. The answer is true if the last stone's set is non-empty.

Complexity is O(n²) in the worst case, which is fine for n = 2000.

Positions go up to 2^31 - 1, so `stones[i] + step` can overflow an int. I use `long` for the sum.

A quick early check: if `stones[1] != 1`, return false. The general DP already handles this, because from stone 0 with k = 0 only step 1 is allowed.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public bool CanCross(int[] stones) {
        int n = stones.Length;
        var idx = new Dictionary<long, int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) idx[stones[i]] = i;
        var sets = new HashSet<int>[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) sets[i] = new HashSet<int>();
        sets[0].Add(0);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            foreach (int k in sets[i]) {
                for (int d = -1; d <= 1; d++) {
                    int step = k + d;
                    if (step <= 0) continue;
                    long pos = (long)stones[i] + step;
                    int j;
                    if (idx.TryGetValue(pos, out j)) {
                        sets[j].Add(step);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return sets[n - 1].Count > 0;
    }
}
```

