traces.inference_trace:
For each point i, count the other points by the normalized direction (dx, dy) from i to them. Dividing by the gcd and fixing the sign makes equal slopes map to the same key. The answer is the largest count for any direction, plus 1 for point i itself.

Normalization: g = gcd(|dx|, |dy|), then dx /= g and dy /= g. If dx < 0, or dx == 0 and dy < 0, negate both. Since the points are unique, (dx, dy) is never (0, 0), so g is never 0.

The key packs both values into one long: (dx + 20001) * 100000 + (dy + 20001). After normalization dx and dy lie in [-20000, 20000], so the offset values fit in [1, 40001] and the packing is collision-free.

If n <= 2, the answer is n. Otherwise take the maximum over all i, which is O(n²) overall.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int MaxPoints(int[][] points) {
        int n = points.Length;
        if (n <= 2) return n;
        int best = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            var d = new Dictionary<long, int>();
            int local = 0;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                int dx = points[j][0] - points[i][0];
                int dy = points[j][1] - points[i][1];
                int g = Gcd(System.Math.Abs(dx), System.Math.Abs(dy));
                dx /= g;
                dy /= g;
                if (dx < 0 || (dx == 0 && dy < 0)) {
                    dx = -dx;
                    dy = -dy;
                }
                long key = (long)(dx + 20001) * 100000L + (dy + 20001);
                int c;
                d.TryGetValue(key, out c);
                c++;
                d[key] = c;
                if (c > local) local = c;
            }
            if (local + 1 > best) best = local + 1;
        }
        return best;
    }

    private int Gcd(int a, int b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            int t = a % b;
            a = b;
            b = t;
        }
        return a;
    }
}
```

