With n ≤ 100, brute force over all start positions works. For each start `i`, extend `j` rightward. Stop as soon as a non-vowel appears. Track which of the five vowels have been seen in a bitmask, and add 1 to the count whenever the mask is full (31).

The example inputs in the statement contain trailing spaces, which are non-vowels and so simply end a run, which is the correct behavior.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int CountVowelSubstrings(string word) {
        int count = 0;
        int n = word.Length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int mask = 0;
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                int idx = "aeiou".IndexOf(word[j]);
                if (idx < 0) break;
                mask |= 1 << idx;
                if (mask == 31) count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

