Greedy two-pointer approach: keep `lo = 0` and `hi = n`. For each character, if it is `'I'`, assign `lo` and increment it. If it is `'D'`, assign `hi` and decrement it. After the loop, `lo == hi`, so assign that value to the last position.

This works because choosing `lo` for `'I'` guarantees the next value is larger (everything remaining is at least `lo + 1`), and choosing `hi` for `'D'` guarantees the next value is smaller.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int[] DiStringMatch(string s) {
        int n = s.Length;
        int lo = 0, hi = n;
        int[] res = new int[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (s[i] == 'I') res[i] = lo++;
            else res[i] = hi--;
        }
        res[n] = lo;
        return res;
    }
}
```

