Use a sliding window over the fruit positions. For a window covering positions [L, R], the minimum number of steps is (R-L) + min(|startPos-L|, |startPos-R|).

Keep a left pointer `l` and a running sum. For each right index `r`, add its amount to the sum. While the window's cost exceeds k, subtract the amount at `l` and advance `l`. After that, update the answer with the sum.

The cost formula (R-L) + min(|s-L|, |s-R|) is valid even when the start is outside the window. If s ≤ L, the cost is R-s, which is (R-L)+(L-s) and matches the formula. If s ≥ R, the cost is s-L, which is (R-L)+(s-R) and also matches. If s is inside the window, the formula gives the usual go-one-side-then-the-other cost.

The window only needs to shrink, never grow back. When `l` advances, the cost for a fixed `r` decreases, so the while loop is correct. The cost is not monotone in `l` for a fixed `r` in general, but a window that is too costly for (l, r) is also too costly for (l, r+1) for any larger r. To see this, note that the cost with r+1 is at least the cost with r, since the span grows and the min term does not shrink by more than the span grows. So advancing `l` permanently is safe.

If `l` passes `r`, the window is empty and the sum is 0. Loop with `l <= r` as the condition. When l == r+1 the sum is 0 and the loop stops.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MaxTotalFruits(int[][] fruits, int startPos, int k) {
        int n = fruits.Length;
        int l = 0;
        long sum = 0;
        long best = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
            sum += fruits[r][1];
            while (l <= r) {
                int L = fruits[l][0];
                int R = fruits[r][0];
                int cost = (R - L) + Math.Min(Math.Abs(startPos - L), Math.Abs(startPos - R));
                if (cost > k) {
                    sum -= fruits[l][1];
                    l++;
                } else break;
            }
            if (sum > best) best = sum;
        }
        return (int)best;
    }
}
```

