Approach: reverse the graph and run a topological sort (Kahn's algorithm) starting from the terminal nodes.

1. For each node, set its out-degree to `graph[i].Length`.
2. For every edge `i -> j`, add `i` to the reverse adjacency list of `j`.
3. Put all nodes with out-degree 0 into a queue.
4. Pop a node, mark it safe, and decrement the out-degree of each of its predecessors. Any predecessor that reaches out-degree 0 goes into the queue.
5. Collect the safe nodes in index order, which gives an ascending result.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public IList<int> EventualSafeNodes(int[][] graph) {
        int n = graph.Length;
        var rev = new List<int>[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) rev[i] = new List<int>();
        int[] outd = new int[n];
        var q = new Queue<int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            outd[i] = graph[i].Length;
            foreach (int j in graph[i]) rev[j].Add(i);
            if (outd[i] == 0) q.Enqueue(i);
        }
        bool[] safe = new bool[n];
