- The cost function `a[p-1]*p - prefix[p]` is monotone non-decreasing in `p`, so binary search works. Since `m` is computed as `(rem + prefix[p]) / p` and then capped, it is a valid minimum. Because `p` is the largest feasible index, `m` does not exceed `a[p]`, so the formula is right.
- The first `n-k` sorted values are all `<= target`. If some of them equal `target` (already complete), then `m` capped at `target-1` could be wrong, since the minimum might truly be `target` when all of the first `n-k` are complete. That case is handled by iterating `k` from the count of already-complete gardens upward: for `k < cnt`, the cost formula still works, but the "incomplete" set would contain complete gardens. So start `k` at `cnt` (the number of capped values equal to `target`). Then the first `n-k` values are all `< target`, and `m <= target-1` is correct.

**Beauty.** `k*full + m*partial`, where `m*partial` applies only when `k < n`. Take the maximum over all `k`.

**Binary search detail.** With `len = n-k >= 1`, find the largest `p` in `[1, len]` with `a[p-1]*p - prefix[p] <= rem`. `p = 1` always works, since its cost is 0. Use `long` throughout.

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution {
    public long MaximumBeauty(int[] flowers, long newFlowers, int target, int full, int partial) {
        int n = flowers.Length;
        long[] a = new long[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) a[i] = Math.Min(flowers[i], target);
        Array.Sort(a);
        long[] prefix = new long[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + a[i];
        int cnt = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (a[i] >= target) cnt++;
        long total = prefix[n];
        long best = 0;
        for (int k = cnt; k <= n; k++) {
            long cost = (long)k * target - (total - prefix[n - k]);
            if (cost > newFlowers) continue;
            long rem = newFlowers - cost;
            long beauty = (long)k * full;
            if (k < n) {
                int len = n - k;
                int lo = 1, hi = len;
                while (lo < hi) {
                    int mid = (lo + hi + 1) / 2;
                    long c = a[mid - 1] * mid - prefix[mid];
                    if (c <= rem) lo = mid; else hi = mid - 1;
                }
                long m = (rem + prefix[lo]) / lo;
                if (m > target - 1) m = target - 1;
                beauty += m * partial;
            }
            if (beauty > best) best = beauty;
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

