Mark the broken letters in a boolean array, then scan `text` once. Keep a flag for whether the current word is still typeable. At each space, count the word if the flag is still set, then reset it. Count the last word after the loop.

The examples show trailing spaces (e.g. "hello world "), so the scan must handle them. A trailing space ends the last word, which is counted at that space. The end-of-string step must then not count an empty word, so I track whether the current word has any characters.

Implementation:
- `broken[c - 'a'] = true` for each broken letter; skip any non-lowercase characters such as the space in "ad ".
- `ok = true`, `has = false`, `count = 0`.
- For each character `c`: if it is a space, then if `has && ok` increment `count`, and reset `ok = true`, `has = false`. Otherwise set `has = true`, and if `c` is a lowercase letter and `broken[c - 'a']`, set `ok = false`.
- After the loop, if `has && ok`, increment `count`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int CanBeTypedWords(string text, string brokenLetters) {
        bool[] broken = new bool[26];
        foreach (char c in brokenLetters) {
            if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') broken[c - 'a'] = true;
        }
        int count = 0;
        bool ok = true, has = false;
        foreach (char c in text) {
            if (c == ' ') {
                if (has && ok) count++;
                ok = true;
                has = false;
            } else {
                has = true;
                if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z' && broken[c - 'a']) ok = false;
            }
        }
        if (has && ok) count++;
        return count;
    }
}
```

