This uses the patience-sorting approach, which runs in O(n log n).

Keep an array `tails` where `tails[i]` is the smallest possible tail value of a strictly increasing subsequence of length `i+1`. For each number, binary search for the first position in `tails` whose value is `>= x` (a lower bound, which handles strictness). Replace that entry with `x`, or append `x` if no such position exists. The final length of `tails` is the answer.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int LengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        int[] tails = new int[nums.Length];
        int len = 0;
        foreach (int x in nums) {
            int lo = 0, hi = len;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
                if (tails[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1;
                else hi = mid;
            }
            tails[lo] = x;
            if (lo == len) len++;
        }
        return len;
    }
}
```

