Sort the words by length, then run a DP over a dictionary that maps each word to the length of the longest chain ending at it. For each word, try deleting each character in turn. If the shortened string is in the dictionary, the chain ending at the current word can be one longer than the chain ending at that shorter string. The answer is the maximum value over all words.

The examples contain trailing spaces inside the strings, such as `"a "`. Those spaces are just part of the string and don't affect the logic, since deleting one character still works the same way.

Duplicate words are not an issue: the dictionary is indexed by word, so a repeated word simply overwrites its own entry.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int LongestStrChain(string[] words) {
        var sorted = (string[])words.Clone();
        Array.Sort(sorted, (a, b) => a.Length.CompareTo(b.Length));
        var dp = new Dictionary<string, int>();
        int best = 1;
        foreach (var w in sorted) {
            int cur = 1;
            for (int i = 0; i < w.Length; i++) {
                string p = w.Substring(0, i) + w.Substring(i + 1);
                if (dp.TryGetValue(p, out int v)) {
                    if (v + 1 > cur) cur = v + 1;
                }
            }
            dp[w] = cur;
            if (cur > best) best = cur;
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

