Use a DP over time: `dp[t][v]` is the minimum cost to be at city `v` at exactly time `t`. Since every edge takes at least 1 minute, transitions only go from smaller `t` to larger `t`, so iterating `t` in increasing order is safe.

1. Set `dp[0][0] = passingFees[0]`, and everything else to infinity.
2. For each `t` from 1 to `maxTime`, and for each edge `(x, y, w)` with `w <= t`:
   - If `dp[t-w][x]` is finite, update `dp[t][y] = min(dp[t][y], dp[t-w][x] + fee[y])`.
   - Do the same in the other direction, from `y` to `x`.
3. The answer is the minimum of `dp[t][n-1]` over all `t` from 0 to `maxTime`, or -1 if every value is infinity.

The complexity is about 1000 × 1000 × 2 = 2M operations, and the memory is 1001 × 1000 ints, which is fine.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MinCost(int maxTime, int[][] edges, int[] passingFees) {
        int n = passingFees.Length;
        int INF = int.MaxValue / 2;
        int[][] dp = new int[maxTime + 1][];
        for (int t = 0; t <= maxTime; t++) {
            dp[t] = new int[n];
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[t][i] = INF;
        }
        dp[0][0] = passingFees[0];
        for (int t = 1; t <= maxTime; t++) {
            foreach (var e in edges) {
                int x = e[0], y = e[1], w = e[2];
                if (w > t) continue;
                int p = dp[t - w][x];
                if (p < INF && p + passingFees[y] < dp[t][y]) dp[t][y] = p + passingFees[y];
                p = dp[t - w][y];
                if (p < INF && p + passingFees[x] < dp[t][x]) dp[t][x] = p + passingFees[x];
            }
        }
        int ans = INF;
        for (int t = 0; t <= maxTime; t++) ans = Math.Min(ans, dp[t][n - 1]);
        return ans >= INF ? -1 : ans;
    }
}
```

