Count the mismatches against the pattern "0101...", where position i should hold `'0'` if i is even and `'1'` if i is odd. Call this count `c`. Every position that mismatches this pattern matches the opposite pattern "1010...", so the cost of the opposite pattern is `n - c`. The answer is the smaller of the two, `min(c, n - c)`.

The examples in the statement contain trailing spaces, such as "0100 ". The real input should only contain '0' and '1', so I'm assuming the trailing space is a formatting artifact. To be safe, the loop only counts characters that are '0' or '1' and uses that count as `n`. A stray space would then not affect the result, whereas a position-based `n` would be thrown off.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MinOperations(string s) {
        int c = 0, n = 0;
        foreach (char ch in s) {
            if (ch != '0' && ch != '1') continue;
            int expected = n % 2; // pattern 0101...
            if (ch - '0' != expected) c++;
            n++;
        }
        return Math.Min(c, n - c);
    }
}
```

