The answer is always 0, 1, or 2, so the solution checks each case in turn.

1. If the grid already has a number of islands other than 1 (zero or several), the answer is 0.
2. Otherwise, try removing each land cell in turn. If the island count after removal is not 1, the answer is 1. This covers an island of a single cell, since removing it leaves 0 islands.
3. If no single removal disconnects the grid, the answer is 2. Any island can be disconnected with two removals by isolating a corner cell.

The cost is at most 900 cells, each needing a flood fill of up to 900 cells, which is about 810k operations.

The island count uses an iterative DFS with a stack over a visited array. The removal loop sets the cell to 0, counts islands, and restores it.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    int m, n;
    int Count(int[][] g) {
        bool[,] vis = new bool[m, n];
        int cnt = 0;
        int[] dx = {1, -1, 0, 0};
        int[] dy = {0, 0, 1, -1};
        var st = new System.Collections.Generic.Stack<int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (g[i][j] == 1 && !vis[i, j]) {
                    cnt++;
                    vis[i, j] = true;
                    st.Push(i * n + j);
                    while (st.Count > 0) {
                        int c = st.Pop();
                        int x = c / n, y = c % n;
                        for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++) {
                            int nx = x + dx[d], ny = y + dy[d];
                            if (nx >= 0 && ny >= 0 && nx < m && ny < n && g[nx][ny] == 1 && !vis[nx, ny]) {
                                vis[nx, ny] = true;
                                st.Push(nx * n + ny);
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return cnt;
    }

    public int MinDays(int[][] grid) {
        m = grid.Length;
        n = grid[0].Length;
        if (Count(grid) != 1) return 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    grid[i][j] = 0;
                    int c = Count(grid);
                    grid[i][j] = 1;
                    if (c != 1) return 1;
                }
            }
        }
        return 2;
    }
}
```

