Use memoized recursion: f(n) = 1 + min(n%2 + f(n/2), n%3 + f(n/3)), with f(0)=0 and f(1)=1.

Eating one orange at a time until n is divisible by 2 costs n%2 days, then the halving move costs 1 day. The same reasoning applies to 3. Only O(log² n) distinct states are reachable, so a dictionary memo is enough.

The base cases are n=0 → 0 and n=1 → 1. For n=2 the formula gives 1 + min(0 + f(1), 2 + f(0)) = 2, which is correct.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    Dictionary<int,int> memo = new Dictionary<int,int>();
    public int MinDays(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        if (memo.TryGetValue(n, out int v)) return v;
        int r = 1 + System.Math.Min(n % 2 + MinDays(n / 2), n % 3 + MinDays(n / 3));
        memo[n] = r;
        return r;
    }
}
```

