Use prefix sums and try both orderings: the first subarray before the second, and the second before the first.

Define a helper `Calc(L, M)` that returns the best sum of an L-length subarray placed before an M-length subarray. Scan the end index `i` of the M-window, from `L+M` to `n` in prefix-sum terms:

- `P` is the prefix sum array, with `P[i]` the sum of the first `i` elements.
- The M-window ending at `i` has sum `P[i] - P[i-M]`.
- `bestL` is the maximum L-window sum ending at or before `i-M`. When `i` increases to a new value, update `bestL = max(bestL, P[i-M] - P[i-M-L])`.
- The candidate answer is `bestL + (P[i] - P[i-M])`.

The loop runs `i` from `L+M` to `n`. At each step `j = i-M >= L`, so `P[j] - P[j-L]` is valid. Because `bestL` is updated at every `i`, it covers all L-windows ending at or before `j`.

The answer is `max(Calc(firstLen, secondLen), Calc(secondLen, firstLen))`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MaxSumTwoNoOverlap(int[] nums, int firstLen, int secondLen) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        int[] P = new int[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i + 1] = P[i] + nums[i];
        return System.Math.Max(Calc(P, n, firstLen, secondLen), Calc(P, n, secondLen, firstLen));
    }

    private int Calc(int[] P, int n, int L, int M) {
        int bestL = 0;
        int ans = 0;
        for (int i = L + M; i <= n; i++) {
            int j = i - M;
            bestL = System.Math.Max(bestL, P[j] - P[j - L]);
            ans = System.Math.Max(ans, bestL + P[i] - P[j]);
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
```

