The first element is the root. The remaining elements split into those smaller than the root (left subtree) and those larger (right subtree). Each subtree keeps its relative order. The two sequences can be interleaved in C(l+r, l) ways. So

ways(arr) = C(l+r, l) · ways(left) · ways(right).

The answer is ways(nums) − 1, since the original ordering is counted, and the result is taken mod 1e9+7.

For binomials, precompute Pascal's triangle up to 1000 mod p. That is about 1M entries, which is fine. A jagged array of ints works.

The recursion depth is at most 1000, which is fine. Each level builds new lists, so the total cost is O(n²) in the worst case, also fine.

Implementation: `Count(List<int> a)` returns a long. If `a.Count <= 2`, it returns 1, because with 2 elements there is only one arrangement (the root is first and the other is forced). Otherwise it splits into left and right and returns C(n-1, left.Count) · Count(left) · Count(right) mod p.

Check on example 2, [3,4,5,1,2]: the root is 3, left = [1,2], right = [4,5]. C(4,2) = 6, and each subtree contributes 1, giving 6. Subtracting 1 gives 5, which matches.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    const long MOD = 1000000007;
    int[][] C;

    public int NumOfWays(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        C = new int[n + 1][];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            C[i] = new int[i + 1];
            C[i][0] = 1;
            C[i][i] = 1;
            for (int j = 1; j < i; j++) {
                C[i][j] = (int)(((long)C[i - 1][j - 1] + C[i - 1][j]) % MOD);
            }
        }
        long res = Count(new List<int>(nums));
        return (int)(((res - 1) % MOD + MOD) % MOD);
    }

    long Count(List<int> a) {
        int n = a.Count;
        if (n <= 2) return 1;
        int root = a[0];
        var left = new List<int>();
        var right = new List<int>();
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if (a[i] < root) left.Add(a[i]);
            else right.Add(a[i]);
        }
        long r = C[n - 1][left.Count];
        r = r * Count(left) % MOD;
        r = r * Count(right) % MOD;
        return r;
    }
}
```

