traces.inference_trace:
Binary search on the number of days. If `m * k > n`, return -1 (use `long` to avoid overflow).

For a candidate day `d`, scan the garden left to right and keep a count of consecutive flowers with `bloomDay[i] <= d`. When that count reaches `k`, form a bouquet and reset the count. If the count of bouquets reaches `m`, `d` is feasible. A flower that has not bloomed also resets the count.

Feasibility is monotonic in `d`, so binary search between the minimum and maximum of `bloomDay` finds the smallest feasible day.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MinDays(int[] bloomDay, int m, int k) {
        long need = (long)m * k;
        int n = bloomDay.Length;
        if (need > n) return -1;
        int lo = int.MaxValue, hi = int.MinValue;
        foreach (int b in bloomDay) {
            if (b < lo) lo = b;
            if (b > hi) hi = b;
        }
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (Can(bloomDay, m, k, mid)) hi = mid;
            else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }

    private bool Can(int[] b, int m, int k, int d) {
        int count = 0, run = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < b.Length; i++) {
            if (b[i] <= d) {
                run++;
                if (run == k) {
                    count++;
                    run = 0;
                    if (count >= m) return true;
                }
            } else {
                run = 0;
            }
        }
        return count >= m;
    }
}
```

