Start with dp[0] = (1, 0). For each mask and each task i not in it, try adding the task:
- If used + tasks[i] <= sessionTime, the new state is (sessions, used + tasks[i]).
- Otherwise it is (sessions + 1, tasks[i]).

Keep the lexicographically smaller state for each new mask. This is correct because fewer sessions is always at least as good, and for the same session count, less time used in the last session is better.

The answer is dp[full].sessions. With n ≤ 14 this is about 2^14 · 14 operations.

I encode each state as sessions * 100 + used, so integer comparison gives the lexicographic order. Since used ≤ 15 < 100, the encoding is safe.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int MinSessions(int[] tasks, int sessionTime) {
        int n = tasks.Length;
        int full = (1 << n) - 1;
        int[] dp = new int[full + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= full; i++) dp[i] = int.MaxValue;
        dp[0] = 100;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] == int.MaxValue) continue;
            int s = dp[mask] / 100;
            int u = dp[mask] % 100;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << i)) != 0) continue;
                int ns, nu;
                if (u + tasks[i] <= sessionTime) {
                    ns = s;
                    nu = u + tasks[i];
                } else {
                    ns = s + 1;
                    nu = tasks[i];
                }
                int code = ns * 100 + nu;
                int nm = mask | (1 << i);
                if (code < dp[nm]) dp[nm] = code;
            }
        }
        return dp[full] / 100;
    }
}
```

