This is a standard dynamic programming problem. Let `dp[i,j]` be true if the first `i` characters of `s` match the first `j` characters of `p`.

**Base case:** `dp[0,0] = true`.

**Transitions for `j` from 1 to `n`:**
- If `p[j-1] == '*'`, the starred element is `p[j-2]`.
  - Zero occurrences: `dp[i,j] = dp[i,j-2]`.
  - One or more occurrences: if `i > 0` and `p[j-2]` is `'.'` or equals `s[i-1]`, then `dp[i,j]` is also true when `dp[i-1,j]` is true.
- Otherwise, if `i > 0` and `p[j-1]` is `'.'` or equals `s[i-1]`, then `dp[i,j] = dp[i-1,j-1]`.

Row `i = 0` is handled by the same loop, since the zero-occurrence rule lets patterns like `a*b*` match the empty string. The answer is `dp[m,n]`.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool IsMatch(string s, string p) {
        int m = s.Length, n = p.Length;
        var dp = new bool[m + 1, n + 1];
        dp[0, 0] = true;
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (p[j - 1] == '*') {
                    if (j >= 2) {
                        dp[i, j] = dp[i, j - 2];
                        if (!dp[i, j] && i > 0 && (p[j - 2] == '.' || p[j - 2] == s[i - 1])) {
                            dp[i, j] = dp[i - 1, j];
                        }
                    }
                } else {
                    if (i > 0 && (p[j - 1] == '.' || p[j - 1] == s[i - 1])) {
                        dp[i, j] = dp[i - 1, j - 1];
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m, n];
    }
}
```

