I'll use a memoized recursion over (i, j, len), where i is the start in s1, j is the start in s2, and len is the substring length.

For each state, first check that the two substrings are equal (return true) or have different character counts (return false). Otherwise try every split k from 1 to len-1:
- No swap: scramble(i, j, k) and scramble(i+k, j+k, len-k).
- Swap: scramble(i, j+len-k, k) and scramble(i+k, j, len-k).

The memo is a sbyte array of size n·n·(n+1), where 0 means not yet computed, 1 means true, and 2 means false.

The character count check prunes most branches. It costs O(26) or O(len) per state, which is fine for n ≤ 30.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    sbyte[,,] memo;
    string a, b;
    public bool IsScramble(string s1, string s2) {
        if (s1.Length != s2.Length) return false;
        a = s1; b = s2;
        int n = s1.Length;
        memo = new sbyte[n, n, n + 1];
        return Go(0, 0, n);
    }

    bool Go(int i, int j, int len) {
        if (memo[i, j, len] != 0) return memo[i, j, len] == 1;
        bool res = false;
        bool equal = true;
        for (int t = 0; t < len; t++) {
            if (a[i + t] != b[j + t]) { equal = false; break; }
        }
        if (equal) res = true;
        else {
            int[] cnt = new int[26];
            for (int t = 0; t < len; t++) {
                cnt[a[i + t] - 'a']++;
                cnt[b[j + t] - 'a']--;
            }
            bool ok = true;
            for (int t = 0; t < 26; t++) if (cnt[t] != 0) { ok = false; break; }
            if (ok) {
                for (int k = 1; k < len && !res; k++) {
                    if (Go(i, j, k) && Go(i + k, j + k, len - k)) res = true;
                    else if (Go(i, j + len - k, k) && Go(i + k, j, len - k)) res = true;
                }
            }
        }
        memo[i, j, len] = (sbyte)(res ? 1 : 2);
        return res;
    }
}
```

