I'll use a `SortedSet<(long value, int index)>` as an order-statistic structure. C# has no built-in order-statistic tree, so I'll use the two-set approach with a lower and an upper set instead.

- `lo` holds the smaller half and has size (k+1)/2. `hi` holds the larger half.
- Both sets are ordered by (value, index), so every element is unique.
- To add an element, put it in `lo` if it is less than or equal to `lo.Max`. Otherwise put it in `hi`. Then rebalance.
- To remove an element, check which set contains it, remove it from there, and rebalance.
- Rebalance: while `lo.Count > (k+1)/2`, move `lo.Max` to `hi`. While `lo.Count < (k+1)/2` and `hi` is non-empty, move `hi.Min` to `lo`.
- The median is `lo.Max` if k is odd. Otherwise it is `(lo.Max + hi.Min) / 2.0`, computed in long to avoid overflow.

The tuple comparison is the default for `ValueTuple<long,int>`, so I can use `new SortedSet<(long,int)>()` directly. `Max` and `Min` on a `SortedSet` are O(log n), which is fine.

For each i, add `nums[i]`. Once i >= k-1, record the median, then remove `nums[i-k+1]`.

During removal, the window has k+1 elements if I remove after adding. To keep the sizes right, I process in this order: for i >= k, remove `nums[i-k]` first, then add `nums[i]`. Within the loop, the target size for `lo` is (k+1)/2 whenever the window is full. Before the window is full the rebalance would use the same target, which would be wrong. So I use a rule that works for any size, with the target for `lo` being (total+1)/2 where total = lo.Count + hi.Count.

After removal, the total is k-1 and the target is k/2. After adding, the total is k and the target is (k+1)/2. The rebalance computes the target from the current total, so this works.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    SortedSet<(long, int)> lo = new SortedSet<(long, int)>();
    SortedSet<(long, int)> hi = new SortedSet<(long, int)>();

    void Rebalance() {
        int total = lo.Count + hi.Count;
        int target = (total + 1) / 2;
        while (lo.Count > target) {
            var m = lo.Max;
            lo.Remove(m);
            hi.Add(m);
        }
        while (lo.Count < target) {
            var m = hi.Min;
            hi.Remove(m);
            lo.Add(m);
        }
    }

    public double[] MedianSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        var res = new double[n - k + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (i >= k) {
                var r = ((long)nums[i - k], i - k);
                if (!lo.Remove(r)) hi.Remove(r);
                Rebalance();
            }
            var e = ((long)nums[i], i);
            if (lo.Count == 0 || e.Item1 < lo.Max.Item1 || (e.Item1 == lo.Max.Item1 && e.Item2 <= lo.Max.Item2))
                lo.Add(e);
            else
                hi.Add(e);
            Rebalance();
            if (i >= k - 1) {
                if (k % 2 == 1) res[i - k + 1] = lo.Max.Item1;
                else res[i - k + 1] = ((double)lo.Max.Item1 + hi.Min.Item1) / 2.0;
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

