This uses a bitmask DP over subsets of `nums`.

1. Compute the total sum. If it is not divisible by `k`, return false. Otherwise set `target = sum / k`.
2. If any element is greater than `target`, return false.
3. Let `dp[mask]` be the sum of the elements in `mask` modulo `target`, or -1 if `mask` is unreachable. Start with `dp[0] = 0`.
4. For each reachable `mask` and each index `i` not in `mask`, let `nm = mask | (1 << i)`. If `dp[mask] + nums[i] <= target` and `nm` has not been reached yet, set `dp[nm] = (dp[mask] + nums[i]) % target`.
5. Because every element is at most `target`, filling a group exactly resets the running sum to 0 and the next element starts a new group. The answer is `dp[full] == 0`, i.e. `dp[full] != -1`.

Since `dp[mask]` stores the sum modulo `target`, the check `dp[mask] + nums[i] <= target` is exactly the condition that the current group is not overfilled. The state is well defined because the total of `mask` determines the remainder, so any order of adding elements that stays valid gives the same value.

With n = 16 this is 65536 × 16 operations, which is fast.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool CanPartitionKSubsets(int[] nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        int sum = 0;
        foreach (int x in nums) sum += x;
        if (sum % k != 0) return false;
        int target = sum / k;
        foreach (int x in nums) if (x > target) return false;
        int full = (1 << n) - 1;
        int[] dp = new int[1 << n];
        for (int i = 0; i < dp.Length; i++) dp[i] = -1;
        dp[0] = 0;
        for (int mask = 0; mask <= full; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] < 0) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << i)) != 0) continue;
                int nm = mask | (1 << i);
                if (dp[nm] != -1) continue;
                if (dp[mask] + nums[i] <= target) {
                    dp[nm] = (dp[mask] + nums[i]) % target;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[full] == 0;
    }
}
```

