Binary search on the answer. The lower bound is the maximum element and the upper bound is the total sum. For a candidate limit `mid`, greedily count how many subarrays are needed so that no subarray sum exceeds `mid`. If that count is at most `k`, then `mid` is feasible and we try smaller; otherwise we try larger.

Using fewer than `k` subarrays is fine, because a subarray can always be split further without increasing the maximum. This works as long as `k <= n`, which the constraints guarantee. Zeros in the array are handled correctly.

The sum can reach 1000 × 10^6 = 10^9, which fits in an int, but I use long to be safe.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int SplitArray(int[] nums, int k) {
        long lo = 0, hi = 0;
        foreach (int x in nums) {
            if (x > lo) lo = x;
            hi += x;
        }
        while (lo < hi) {
            long mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
            int count = 1;
            long cur = 0;
            foreach (int x in nums) {
                if (cur + x > mid) {
                    count++;
                    cur = x;
                } else {
                    cur += x;
                }
            }
            if (count <= k) hi = mid;
            else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        return (int)lo;
    }
}
```

