traces.inference_trace:
Build a trie of the reversed words. Keep the stream's characters in a buffer, and on each query walk the trie from the newest character backwards, returning true as soon as a word-end node is reached.

The example input shows words with trailing spaces (e.g. "cd "), but this is a formatting artifact. The real constraints say words are lowercase letters, so I treat them as such. To be safe against stray characters, the trie uses 26 children and skips any character outside 'a'..'z' when inserting.

The walk never needs to go back further than the longest word (200), so the buffer is capped at that length. A `List<char>` would work, but a StringBuilder-like array is simpler: store the stream in a `char[]` of size maxLen as a circular buffer, or just append to a list and walk back at most maxLen characters. At most 4·10^4 queries × 200 steps is fine.

Each trie node has `Node[] next = new Node[26]` and a `bool end`. In `Query`, append the letter, then start at the root and iterate i from the last index down to max(0, count − maxLen). At each step, move to `next[c - 'a']`, return false if it is null, and return true if the node is an end.

If a word contained a non-letter character, I would drop that character. That can't happen under the constraints, so I only make sure nothing crashes.

```csharp
public class StreamChecker {

    private class Node {
        public Node[] next = new Node[26];
        public bool end;
    }

    private Node root = new Node();
    private List<char> stream = new List<char>();
    private int maxLen = 0;

    public StreamChecker(string[] words) {
        foreach (var w in words) {
            Node cur = root;
            int len = 0;
            for (int i = w.Length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
                int c = w[i] - 'a';
                if (c < 0 || c >= 26) continue;
                len++;
                if (cur.next[c] == null) cur.next[c] = new Node();
                cur = cur.next[c];
            }
            if (len > 0) {
                cur.end = true;
                if (len > maxLen) maxLen = len;
            }
        }
    }

    public bool Query(char letter) {
        stream.Add(letter);
        Node cur = root;
        int lo = Math.Max(0, stream.Count - maxLen);
        for (int i = stream.Count - 1; i >= lo; i--) {
            int c = stream[i] - 'a';
            if (c < 0 || c >= 26) return false;
            cur = cur.next[c];
            if (cur == null) return false;
            if (cur.end) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

