traces.inference_trace:
Build an adjacency map from the pairs. In a path, the two endpoints are the only values with exactly one neighbor. Start from one endpoint and walk along the path, never stepping back to the previous element.

Each value has at most 2 neighbors, so a `Dictionary<int, List<int>>` works.

Steps:
1. Add each pair `(u, v)` to the adjacency lists of both `u` and `v`.
2. Find a key whose list has exactly one neighbor and use it as `start`.
3. Set `res[0] = start`. For `i >= 1`, take the neighbor of `res[i-1]` that differs from `res[i-2]`. For `i = 1`, there is no previous element, so take the only neighbor.
4. Because values are unique, comparing against the previous element is enough to avoid stepping back.

Here `n = adjacentPairs.Length + 1`.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int[] RestoreArray(int[][] adjacentPairs) {
        var adj = new Dictionary<int, List<int>>();
        foreach (var p in adjacentPairs) {
            if (!adj.ContainsKey(p[0])) adj[p[0]] = new List<int>();
            if (!adj.ContainsKey(p[1])) adj[p[1]] = new List<int>();
            adj[p[0]].Add(p[1]);
            adj[p[1]].Add(p[0]);
        }
        int n = adjacentPairs.Length + 1;
        int start = 0;
        foreach (var kv in adj) {
            if (kv.Value.Count == 1) { start = kv.Key; break; }
        }
        int[] res = new int[n];
        res[0] = start;
        res[1] = adj[start][0];
        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
            var list = adj[res[i - 1]];
            res[i] = list[0] == res[i - 2] ? list[1] : list[0];
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

