traces.inference_trace:
Sort the products with ordinal comparison. For each prefix length, collect up to three products that start with that prefix.

Simple approach: sort once, then for each prefix, binary search for the lower bound and take up to 3 entries from there that match the prefix. A linear scan would also be fast enough: 1000 prefixes × 1000 products × a prefix check is about 10^6 operations, each costing at most the prefix length. The sum of lengths is bounded, so a binary search is safer and not much harder.

Implementation: sort with `string.CompareOrdinal`. For each i, set prefix = searchWord.Substring(0, i+1). Binary search for the first index with `CompareOrdinal(products[idx], prefix) >= 0`. Then, from that index, take up to 3 products while `StartsWith(prefix, StringComparison.Ordinal)` holds.

The examples show trailing spaces in the strings, but this is just formatting, so I ignore it.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public IList<IList<string>> SuggestedProducts(string[] products, string searchWord) {
        Array.Sort(products, string.CompareOrdinal);
        var res = new List<IList<string>>();
        int n = products.Length;
        for (int i = 0; i < searchWord.Length; i++) {
            string prefix = searchWord.Substring(0, i + 1);
            int lo = 0, hi = n;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
                if (string.CompareOrdinal(products[mid], prefix) >= 0) hi = mid;
                else lo = mid + 1;
            }
            var list = new List<string>();
            for (int j = lo; j < n && list.Count < 3; j++) {
                if (products[j].StartsWith(prefix, StringComparison.Ordinal)) list.Add(products[j]);
                else break;
            }
            res.Add(list);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

