A word is uncommon exactly when it appears once in the combined text of both sentences. If it appears once in total, it is in one sentence only and only once there. If it appears more than once in total, it either repeats within one sentence or appears in both, so it is not uncommon.

So I count every word across `s1` and `s2` together and return the words whose count is 1.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public string[] UncommonFromSentences(string s1, string s2) {
        var counts = new Dictionary<string, int>();
        foreach (var w in (s1 + " " + s2).Split(' ')) {
            if (w.Length == 0) continue;
            counts.TryGetValue(w, out int c);
            counts[w] = c + 1;
        }
        var res = new List<string>();
        foreach (var kv in counts) {
            if (kv.Value == 1) res.Add(kv.Key);
        }
        return res.ToArray();
    }
