traces.inference_trace:
This uses the greedy two-pointer approach with backtracking to the most recent `*`.

1. Walk through `s` with index `i` and through `p` with index `j`. Remember the position of the last `*` seen (`star`) and the index in `s` where it started matching (`match`).
2. If `j < p.Length` and `p[j]` is `?` or equals `s[i]`, advance both pointers.
3. If `p[j]` is `*`, record `star = j` and `match = i`, then advance `j` only. This first lets the `*` match the empty sequence.
4. If the characters don't match and there is a previous `*`, backtrack: set `j = star + 1`, increment `match`, and set `i = match`. This extends what the `*` consumes by one character.
5. If there is no previous `*`, return false.
6. After `s` is consumed, skip any trailing `*` in `p`. The match succeeds only if `j` reaches the end of `p`.

The loop runs in O(n·m) time in the worst case and uses O(1) extra space.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public bool IsMatch(string s, string p) {
        int i = 0, j = 0, star = -1, match = 0;
        int n = s.Length, m = p.Length;
        while (i < n) {
            if (j < m && (p[j] == '?' || p[j] == s[i])) {
                i++;
                j++;
            } else if (j < m && p[j] == '*') {
                star = j;
                match = i;
                j++;
            } else if (star != -1) {
                j = star + 1;
                match++;
                i = match;
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }
        while (j < m && p[j] == '*') j++;
        return j == m;
    }
}
```

