traces.inference_trace:
Approach: breadth-first search from `beginWord`. For each word, try replacing each character with 'a' through 'z' and check whether the result is in a HashSet built from `wordList`. Remove a word from the set when it is visited so it is never processed twice. Return the level count when `endWord` is reached.

If `endWord` is not in the set, return 0 immediately.

The BFS starts with `beginWord` at level 1. For each dequeued word, each neighbor found in the set is removed from it and enqueued with level + 1. If that neighbor equals `endWord`, return its level.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int LadderLength(string beginWord, string endWord, IList<string> wordList) {
        var set = new HashSet<string>(wordList);
        if (!set.Contains(endWord)) return 0;
        var q = new Queue<string>();
        q.Enqueue(beginWord);
        set.Remove(beginWord);
        int level = 1;
        while (q.Count > 0) {
            int n = q.Count;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                string w = q.Dequeue();
                if (w == endWord) return level;
                char[] arr = w.ToCharArray();
                for (int j = 0; j < arr.Length; j++) {
                    char orig = arr[j];
                    for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; c++) {
                        if (c == orig) continue;
                        arr[j] = c;
                        string nw = new string(arr);
                        if (set.Remove(nw)) {
                            q.Enqueue(nw);
                        }
                    }
                    arr[j] = orig;
                }
            }
            level++;
        }
        return 0;
    }
}
```

